9. After receipt of above judgment of the Hon’ble High Court, this Bench of the Tribunal has provided a fresh opportunity of being heard to both the parties. 10. Shri Anadi Verma, learned senior Departmental Representative submitted that the amount of Rs. 29,48,843 has not been received by the assessee till today and therefore, it was his duty to inform the Assessing Officer during the course of assessment proceedings or the penalty proceedings, but no such attempt has been made, as such, the intention of the assessee was mala fide, hence penalty was leviable under section 271(1)(c) of the Act. Shri Anadi Verma, learned senior Departmental Representative relied on the judgment, of Hon’ble Jharkhand High Court in the case of CIT v. Mahabit Prasad Bajaj [2008] 298 ITR 109 wherein it has been held that concealment means an attempt to hide an item of income or a portion thereof from the knowledge of the IT authorities, Shri Anadi Verma, learned Departmental Representative also relied on the judgment of Hon’ble High Court of Allahabad in the case of Raj Kumar Chaurasia v. CIT [2008] 172 Taxman 365 (All.). In the said case, in penalty proceedings, the assessee neither gave any material or evidence nor offered any other plausible explanation to show that there was no gross or wilful neglect on its part and there was no fraud in not disclosing the correct income, the Hon’ble High Court held that penalty under section 271(1)(c) of the Act was rightly imposed upon the assessee. The learned Departmental Representative also relied on the decision of Hon’ble Allahabad High Court in the ease of Balwant Rai & Co. v. CIT [2005] 274 ITR 269 1 (All.) wherein it has been held that the assessee did not offer any explanation regarding the source of deposit of Rs. 13,000 in the assessment proceedings or in the penalty proceedings. Hence, in view of clause (A) of Explanation 1 to section 271(1)(c), the amount of Rs. 13,000 had to be deemed to represent the income of the assessee in respect of which particulars had been concealed, the Hon’ble High Court has held that therefore the Tribunal was justified In invoking the provisions of Explanation as they stood on 10-2-1978, i.e., the date of filing the return, Shri Anadi Verma, learned Departmental Representative relied upon the decision of Tribunal, Calcutta Bench in the case of Asstt. CIT v. Mcleod Russel (I) Ltd. [2006] 101 ITD 39 (Kol.) in which it has been held that claiming of excess deduction also amounts to concealment of income, Shri Anadi Verma, learned senior Departmental Representative also relied on the following observations of the Tribunal, Pune Bench ‘B’ in the case of Proj. Industries Ltd. v. Jt. CIT [2009] 118 ITD 447 (Pune), the Pune Bench of the Tribunal has referred to its earlier decision in the case of Asstt. CIT v. Malhotra Mukesh Satpal [2008] 115 ITD 467 (Pune) wherein the Tribunal has concluded and summarized the scope and effect of section 271(1)(c) read with Explanation 1 as under :

